To check for an email in both the mail field and identities (e.g., external or other associated emails), you can query the Azure AD B2C identities or other properties like identities.

Azure AD uses the identities collection to store multiple identities, such as emails from external providers. Here’s how to check both mail, userPrincipalName, and identities.


---

Updated Code (Including Identities)

using Microsoft.Graph;
using Azure.Identity;
using System.Linq;
using System;

class Program
{
    static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Azure AD App details
        var clientId = "YOUR_CLIENT_ID";
        var tenantId = "YOUR_TENANT_ID";
        var clientSecret = "YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET";

        // Authenticate using ClientSecretCredential
        var credential = new ClientSecretCredential(tenantId, clientId, clientSecret);

        // Initialize GraphServiceClient
        var graphClient = new GraphServiceClient(credential);

        // Email to check
        var emailToCheck = "user@example.com";

        try
        {
            // Query Azure AD users
            var users = await graphClient.Users
                .Request()
                .Filter($"mail eq '{emailToCheck}' or userPrincipalName eq '{emailToCheck}'")
                .GetAsync();

            // Check identities if no direct match is found
            if (users.CurrentPage.Any())
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Email found in mail or userPrincipalName: {emailToCheck}");
            }
            else
            {
                // Fetch all users and check their identities (fallback)
                var allUsers = await graphClient.Users
                    .Request()
                    .Select("id,displayName,identities") // Fetch only relevant fields
                    .GetAsync();

                var matchedUser = allUsers.CurrentPage.FirstOrDefault(user =>
                    user.Identities != null && user.Identities.Any(identity => identity.IssuerAssignedId == emailToCheck));

                if (matchedUser != null)
                {
                    Console.WriteLine($"Email found in identities: {emailToCheck}");
                }
                else
                {
                    Console.WriteLine($"Email does not exist: {emailToCheck}");
                }
            }
        }
        catch (ServiceException ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Error: {ex.Message}");
        }
    }
}


---

Key Points:

1. Primary Search:

The $filter query checks mail and userPrincipalName.

This is efficient as it only queries specific fields.



2. Fallback Search:

If no direct match is found, fetch identities (e.g., external emails like Gmail, Microsoft accounts).

Use Select("id,displayName,identities") to limit the fields retrieved for better performance.



3. Check Identities Field:

Identities is a collection where each entry includes:

Issuer: The identity provider (e.g., local, google.com).

IssuerAssignedId: The email or username for that provider.






---

Azure AD Permissions:

User.Read.All is required for accessing users and their identities.

Grant admin consent for application-level permissions.



